{"obligations":{"reduction":{"status":"written_and_valid","quote":null,"witness":null,"load_bearing":null},"lower_bound":{"status":"written_but_invalid","quote":"If all prime factors of \(x\) are at most \(A\), then \(\sigma(x)=P(x)\), which is good by (1).","witness":"Counterexample: take A=15. The sequence begins 15,18,20: 16 and 17 are coprime to 15, so 18 is next; 19 fails, while 20 shares 5 with 15 and 2 with 18. For x=18 all prime factors are at most 15, but σ(18)={3}≠{2,3}=P(18), and {3} is not good because it misses P(20)={2,5}.","load_bearing":"The proof needs every selected term to retain goodness after deleting primes outside Q. This lemma is the sole basis for the forward implication in (3)."},"upper_bound":{"status":"written_but_invalid","quote":"Hence \(\sigma(m+L)=\sigma(m)\), and (3) gives
\[
m\text{ is a sequence term}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
m+L\text{ is a sequence term}. \tag{4}
\]","witness":"Counterexample: for A=15, the defined Q is {3,5}, so L=15. As verified above, 18 and 20 are sequence terms. But 33 is not a sequence term because gcd(33,20)=1. Thus 18 is a term while 18+L=33 is not, directly contradicting (4).","load_bearing":"The fixed block count and the assertion that a_n+L is a term both rely on (4). Without this translation equivalence, the claimed universal index shift does not follow."}},"S_valid":0,"wrong_answer_stated":false,"notes":"Mapping: reduction is the static scan characterization (1); lower_bound is erasure to a finite prime trace; upper_bound is two-way translation and block counting. The fatal issue is that Q contains primes dividing A, not all primes at most A."}
